Other notes: 
In this case, the expooo exploit used a Race Condition (as seen in /.trash/switch_race.c). The goal of that race condition was likely to gain a split-second of root-level access—just long enough to reach into /etc/pam.d/ and swap a single word (required to permit). Once they changed the configuration to permit, they didn't need the exploit anymore; they could just type su and the system would let them in forever.

Think of PAM (Pluggable Authentication Modules) as the "bouncer" for the macOS operating system. Whenever you try to do something that requires permission—like logging in, using sudo, or changing your password—the system doesn't just check one thing; it goes to the /etc/pam.d/ folder and reads a "checklist" (a configuration file) to decide if you should be allowed in.
